     1	--- Page 1 ---
     2	Contents lists available at ScienceDirect
     3	Information and Software Technology
     4	journal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/infsof
     5	On the road to interactive LLM-based systematic mapping studies
     6	Kai Petersena,b,∗,Jan M. Gerkenb
     7	aBlekinge Institute of Technology, Valhallavägen 1, 37141 Karlskrona, Sweden
     8	bFlensburg University of Applied Sciences (FUAS), Kanzleistraße 91-93, 24943 Flensburg, Germany
     9	A R T I C L E I N F O
    10	Keywords:
    11	Systematic mapping studies
    12	Large language models
    13	GPTA B S T R A C T
    14	Context: The research volume is continuously increasing. Manual analysis of large topic scopes and continu-
    15	ously updating literature studies with the newest research results is effort intensive and, therefore, difficult to
    16	achieve.
    17	Objective: To discuss possibilities and next steps for using LLMs (e.g., GPT-4) in the mapping study process.
    18	Method: The research can be classified as a solution proposal. The solution was iteratively designed and
    19	discussed among the authors based on their experience with LLMs and literature reviews.
    20	Results: We propose strategies for the mapping process, outlining the use of agents and prompting strategies
    21	for each step.
    22	Conclusion: Given the potential of LLMs in literature studies, we should work on a holistic solutions for
    23	LLM-supported mapping studies.
    24	1. Introduction
    25	The mapping  study methodology  is frequently  used in the context  of
    26	software  engineering.  Two guidelines  for mapping  studies  (MSs) were
    27	proposed  and are, to date, cited more than 7000 times.1The purpose  of
    28	mapping  studies  is to classify  research  and observe  trends.  In contrast,
    29	systematic  literature  reviews  (SLRs)  focus on synthesizing  evidence.
    30	Given the purpose  of mapping  studies,  a more extensive  set of studies
    31	should  be targeted,  as otherwise,  it would  make sense to conduct  an
    32	SLR directly.
    33	This article  discusses  the possibilities  of using LLMs throughout  the
    34	mapping  process,  proposing  strategies  for using LLMs in each research
    35	step of MSs. There are various  motivations  for using LLMs in MSs: (1)
    36	An increased  number  of published  papers  to be analyzed;  (2) Conduct-
    37	ing mapping  studies  on a larger scope; (3) Getting  additional  research
    38	design  ideas by interacting  with the LLM; (4) Reduced  effort allows
    39	updating  mapping  studies  more regularly.  The solution  described  here
    40	is a direction  for researchers  in the software  community  to work on
    41	solutions  as a community,  as a reliable  and intensively  evaluated  solu-
    42	tion will likely result from a community  effort. This article  may serve
    43	as a starting  point for discussing  using LLMs in MSs, working  towards
    44	a holistic  solution,  and evaluating  the solution  in the SE context.
    45	We describe  the solution  and provide  references  to relevant  studies.
    46	Our solution  is inspired  by [1], where the authors  propose  a sys-
    47	tem where testers  interact  with a search-based  software  test system,
    48	∗Corresponding  author at: Flensburg  University  of Applied  Sciences  (FUAS),  Kanzleistraße  91-93, 24943 Flensburg,  Germany.
    49	E-mail addresses: kai.petersen@hs-flensburg.de ,kai.petersen@bth.se (K. Petersen) ,jan.gerken@hs-flensburg.de (J.M. Gerken) .
    50	1According  to Google  Scholar,  August  2024.observing  the output  and the tester providing  feedback  to the search-
    51	based system  based on candidate  solutions.  In our solution  proposal,  we
    52	follow  this human-in-the-loop  approach  outlined  by [1]. To assess and
    53	react appropriately  to the output  of the LLM-system,  it is essential  that
    54	reviewers  (a) are well educated  in using the mapping  study method,
    55	and (b) be experts  in the topic they are reviewing.
    56	Khalil et al. [2] reviewed  machine  learning  tools for literature  stud-
    57	ies. The tools were assessed  prior to ChatGPT’s  release.  Therefore,  the
    58	tools required  manual  labeling  (e.g. during  paper screening),  whereas
    59	a LLM like ChatGPT  can produce  a descriptive  assessment  outcome  in-
    60	cluding  rationales  and text fragments.  Similarly,  the framework  by [3]
    61	focusing  on paper screening  needed  to be trained  based on the users’
    62	labels.
    63	2. LLM-supported  mapping  process
    64	This section  discusses  how to incorporate  LLMs in every step of the
    65	mapping  process,  which is presented  in [4].Fig. 1presents  each step
    66	of the review  process  and briefly  outlines  the input and actions  done
    67	by the user and the output  of the LLM. For all the steps outlined  below
    68	we would  like to highlight  that the researcher  should  have an initial
    69	idea of possible  search  terms,  inclusion  criteria  or extraction  items in
    70	order to validate  the outputs.  Specifically,  research  problems  and goals
    71	should  be driven  by relevant  practical  needs.
    72	https://doi.org/10.1016/j.infsof.2024.107611
    73	Received  13 June 2024; Received  in revised  form 22 October  2024; Accepted  23 October  2024Information  and Software  Technology  178 (2025)  107611
    74	Available  online  31 October  2024
    75	0950-5849/©  2024  The Authors.  Published  by Elsevier  B.V. This is an open access  article  under  the CC BY license  ( http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/  ).
    76
    77	--- Page 2 ---
    78	K. Petersen  and J.M. Gerken
    79	Fig. 1.The mapping  process with LLM support.
    80	2.1. Establishing  a need for the map
    81	At this stage, we provide  the LLM with our research  objectives
    82	and contextual  information  (e.g., paper abstract)  to elicit proposals
    83	for research  questions  and complementary  information  (e.g., additional
    84	objectives).  We edit the questions  as input for the next stage.
    85	2.2. Study identification
    86	2.2.1. Search
    87	Proposed  strategy: Search  aims to identify  relevant  documents  con-
    88	cerning  the research  objective.  Along with defining  research  objectives,
    89	the search  is a complex  and iterative  process.  Creating  a search  strategy
    90	from a search  intent involves  understanding  the searcher’s  underlying
    91	intentions.  The strategy  must be both transparent  and reproducible.
    92	Even though  semantic  searches2are becoming  more common,  the need
    93	for reproducibility  supports  using Boolean  searches.  Boolean  searches
    94	necessitate  formulating  a search  strategy  using Boolean  expressions  and
    95	operators.  The prerequisite  is the identification  and combination  of
    96	relevant  terms.  We advocate  for an autonomous  agent with Human-in-
    97	the-Loop  to meet the demand  for reproducibility,  account  for human
    98	involvement  in the process,  and leverage  the potential  of new techno-
    99	logical  developments.  We propose  an architecture  that places human
   100	intervention  at the center and considers  three agents:
   101	1.Keyword  Identification  Agent: The Keyword  Identification  Agent
   102	identifies  relevant  terms and provides  them for further  research.
   103	Language  models  can support  the search  for semantically  similar
   104	terms.  Semantic  similarities  can, for instance,  help find terms
   105	commonly  used in the past but have largely  been replaced  by
   106	other terms today.  This step also serves to solidify  the focus of
   107	2Underlined  words are defined  in the supplementary  material.the study. For example,  in 3D printing,  the searcher’s  intention
   108	may be to search  at the conceptual  term level, the level of
   109	subtypes  (e.g., Stereolithography),  or supertypes  (e.g., Rapid
   110	Prototyping).
   111	2.Semantic  Search  Agent: The Semantic  Search  Agent uses a
   112	Retrieval-Augmented  Generation  (RAG) architecture  to suggest
   113	relevant  documents  based on semantic  similarities.  In this con-
   114	text, it may be helpful  to consider  a graph database  as a storage
   115	component,  allowing  for the incorporation  of links between
   116	documents  (e.g., through  citations).  This step does not select
   117	documents  but adjusts  the search  strategy.  Relevant  search  terms
   118	are then extracted  again from the selected  documents.
   119	3.Search  Strategy  Agent: The Search  Strategy  Agent formulates  the
   120	search  strategy  used to conduct  the research.

